            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-56855",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-56855"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-56855?s=golang&n=crypto&ns=golang.org%2Fx&t=golang&vr=%3C0.56.0",
              "help": {
                "text": "Previously, after a channel has been established, a malicious peer could send crafted messages that would deadlock the entire connection.\n\nNow, we handle all RFC 4254 channel messages; global requests are handled explicitly. Then, treat all other messages as a protocol error and tear the connection down instead of buffering and blocking.\n",
                "markdown": "> Previously, after a channel has been established, a malicious peer could send crafted messages that would deadlock the entire connection.\n\nNow, we handle all RFC 4254 channel messages; global requests are handled explicitly. Then, treat all other messages as a protocol error and tear the connection down instead of buffering and blocking.\n\n|                                                       |\n|Package         pkg:golang/golang.org/x/crypto@0.55.0  |\n|Affected range  <0.56.0                                |\n|Fixed version   0.56.0                                 |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<0.56.0",
                "cvssV3_severity": "HIGH",
                "fixed_version": "0.56.0",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:golang/golang.org/x/crypto@0.55.0"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-56866",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-56866"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-56866?s=golang&n=stdlib&t=golang&vr=%3E%3D1.27.0-0%2C%3C1.27.2",
              "help": {
                "text": "When http.Transport sends an HTTP/1 CONNECT request with a non-empty Request.Body, it writes the body directly to the connection without framing after the request headers. If the server rejects the CONNECT request with a non-2xx keep-alive response, Transport returns the connection to the idle pool. Because CONNECT requests do not have a request body, the server may interpret the trailing body bytes as a subsequent pipelined HTTP/1.1 request on the connection, leaving the pooled connection desynchronized and causing the next caller that reuses it to read the response to the injected request. In reverse proxies (including httputil.ReverseProxy) that forward CONNECT requests through a shared Transport, this can lead to cross-user response poisoning.\n",
                "markdown": "> When http.Transport sends an HTTP/1 CONNECT request with a non-empty Request.Body, it writes the body directly to the connection without framing after the request headers. If the server rejects the CONNECT request with a non-2xx keep-alive response, Transport returns the connection to the idle pool. Because CONNECT requests do not have a request body, the server may interpret the trailing body bytes as a subsequent pipelined HTTP/1.1 request on the connection, leaving the pooled connection desynchronized and causing the next caller that reuses it to read the response to the injected request. In reverse proxies (including httputil.ReverseProxy) that forward CONNECT requests through a shared Transport, this can lead to cross-user response poisoning.\n\n|                                          |\n|Package         pkg:golang/stdlib@1.27.1  |\n|Affected range  >=1.27.0-0,<1.27.2        |\n|Fixed version   1.27.2                    |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<1.26.9",
                "cvssV3_severity": "HIGH",
                "fixed_version": "1.26.9",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.26.6",
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.26.7",
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.27.1"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-78659",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-78659"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-78659?s=golang&n=stdlib&t=golang&vr=%3C1.26.9",
              "help": {
                "text": "When \"Trailer\" headers are sent by a client, the HTTP server internally uses the header values to populate the Request.Trailer map passed to the server handler. Because Request.Trailer is a map, each entry incurs memory overhead. For HTTP/2 servers, a malicious client can exploit this by sending a \"Trailer\" header that declares a large number of fields, causing the server to allocate a disproportionate amount of memory while bypassing Server.MaxHeaderValueCount and Server.MaxHeaderBytes limits. This exploit is not applicable for HTTP/1 servers, which do not support multiplexing a large number of requests over one TCP connection, and whose Server.MaxHeaderBytes are calculated differently.\n",
                "markdown": "> When \"Trailer\" headers are sent by a client, the HTTP server internally uses the header values to populate the Request.Trailer map passed to the server handler. Because Request.Trailer is a map, each entry incurs memory overhead. For HTTP/2 servers, a malicious client can exploit this by sending a \"Trailer\" header that declares a large number of fields, causing the server to allocate a disproportionate amount of memory while bypassing Server.MaxHeaderValueCount and Server.MaxHeaderBytes limits. This exploit is not applicable for HTTP/1 servers, which do not support multiplexing a large number of requests over one TCP connection, and whose Server.MaxHeaderBytes are calculated differently.\n\n|                                          |\n|Package         pkg:golang/stdlib@1.26.7  |\n|Affected range  <1.26.9                   |\n|Fixed version   1.26.9                    |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<0.60.0",
                "cvssV3_severity": "HIGH",
                "fixed_version": "0.60.0",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:golang/golang.org/x/net@0.58.0",
                  "pkg:golang/golang.org/x/net@0.59.0",
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.26.6",
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.26.7",
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.27.1"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-78660",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-78660"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-78660?s=golang&n=stdlib&t=golang&vr=%3E%3D1.27.0-0%2C%3C1.27.2",
              "help": {
                "text": "Historically, we have been rather lax about malformed framing-related headers in our HTTP/2 implementation, as they cannot interfere with HTTP/2 framing. However, this makes it possible for our HTTP/2 implementation to forward responses containing such headers to an HTTP/1 client when acting as a reverse proxy. If the HTTP/1 client also does not behave strictly enough, this can result in response smuggling.\n",
                "markdown": "> Historically, we have been rather lax about malformed framing-related headers in our HTTP/2 implementation, as they cannot interfere with HTTP/2 framing. However, this makes it possible for our HTTP/2 implementation to forward responses containing such headers to an HTTP/1 client when acting as a reverse proxy. If the HTTP/1 client also does not behave strictly enough, this can result in response smuggling.\n\n|                                          |\n|Package         pkg:golang/stdlib@1.27.1  |\n|Affected range  >=1.27.0-0,<1.27.2        |\n|Fixed version   1.27.2                    |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<1.26.9",
                "cvssV3_severity": "HIGH",
                "fixed_version": "1.26.9",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:golang/golang.org/x/net@0.58.0",
                  "pkg:golang/golang.org/x/net@0.59.0",
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.26.6",
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.26.7",
                  "pkg:golang/stdlib@1.27.1"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-78662",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-78662"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-78662?s=golang&n=crypto&ns=golang.org%2Fx&t=golang&vr=%3C0.56.0",
              "help": {
                "text": "Previously, a channel registered in the mux's chanList is not usable until it is established. A malicious peer was able flood the channel's incomingRequests, deadlocking the entire connection.\n\nNow, we add an atomic established state, set when a channel becomes usable. Until such a time, handlePacket drops every packet other than the open confirmation/failure, without blocking and without tearing down the connection.\n",
                "markdown": "> Previously, a channel registered in the mux's chanList is not usable until it is established. A malicious peer was able flood the channel's incomingRequests, deadlocking the entire connection.\n\nNow, we add an atomic established state, set when a channel becomes usable. Until such a time, handlePacket drops every packet other than the open confirmation/failure, without blocking and without tearing down the connection.\n\n|                                                       |\n|Package         pkg:golang/golang.org/x/crypto@0.55.0  |\n|Affected range  <0.56.0                                |\n|Fixed version   0.56.0                                 |\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "affected_version": "<0.56.0",
                "cvssV3_severity": "HIGH",
                "fixed_version": "0.56.0",
                "purls": [
                  "pkg:golang/golang.org/x/crypto@0.55.0"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-78667",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-78667"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://scout.docker.com/v/CVE-2026-78667?s=golang&n=stdlib&t=golang&vr=%3C1.26.9",
              "help": {
